{"id": "123130", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123130", "title": "Tijucas", "text": "Tijucas is in Grande Florian\u00f3polis. Its historic central avenues and rural neighborhoods preserve traditional Azorean architectures, multi-generational colonial mansions, and standard fishing customs. It's a city with a friendly atmosphere and Azorean culture. \nUnderstand.\nThe territory of Tijucas was explored by European colonists in the mid-18th century, drawing massive waves of Portuguese immigrants from the Azores Islands who transformed the river basin into a prosperous agricultural and shipping port. The name Tijucas originates from the indigenous Tupi-Guarani word meaning \"swamp\" or \"muddy river,\" referencing the rich clay sediment deposits found at the estuary. \nToday, the town balances its industrial manufacturing identity with a relaxed, small-town lifestyle. In 2019, it was home to 38,000 people. The city is a major powerhouse for the production of high-grade construction ceramics and floor tiles, heavily driven by global corporate brands like Eliane and Portobello.\nThe city has a subtropical climate, with very hot summers and winters where temperatures can reach close to 0 \u00b0C. Frost is common in the months of June and July, especially in the higher areas of the city. The humidity is very high and rarely drops below 60%, even in the driest months.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nTijucas is exceptionally accessible via Brazil's primary coastal transit vein, making road trips straightforward.\nBy bus.\nThe Tijucas Bus Terminal (Terminal Rodovi\u00e1rio de Tijucas), located close to the main highway crossings, is thoroughly integrated into regional transit lines. Major long-distance bus operators (such as Catarinense and Pluma) run highly frequent daily schedules connecting Tijucas directly to Florian\u00f3polis, Itaja\u00ed, and Joinville.\nGet around.\nThe urban city center is relatively flat and straightforward to navigate.\nDo.\nOnce a year, a large festival is held in front of the main church: the Feast of the Holy Spirit. The festival attracts people from the region. During Christmas time, a live nativity scene can even be seen in front of the church. "}
{"id": "123131", "revid": "2196717", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123131", "title": "Lummi Island", "text": "Lummi Island is a 9.25 mi2 island in northwest Washington State, between the mainland and the San Juan Islands. It is best known for its unique reefnet salmon fishery, eclectic population of artists, picturesque seascapes, and rural setting. The island has a population of about 800, which nearly doubles in summer when property owners from both Canada and the U.S. arrive for summer fun and relaxation.\nUnderstand.\nThe Lummi Indian Reservation is situated on a peninsula east of the island, but does not include Lummi Island.\nHistory.\nThe island was called \"Sa nam a o\" (\"High Mountain\") and \"Skallaham\" by the native peoples. In 1792 Spanish explorers dubbed it \"Isla de Pacheco\", and it was later known as McLoughlin Island. In 1853, the U.S. National Geodetic Survey charted the island as \"Lummi\", naming it after the tribe. It is believed the name comes from \"luminara\", referring to the bonfires the Spanish saw upon arrival and whence the tribe also took their name, though there are also theories that it is derived from a native word or phrase.\nThe Island post office came into existence in 1882. At that time mail was addressed to \"Beach Washington\" which was the name of the town. Today Island mail is addressed to \"Lummi Island Washington\", though the Beach School and Beach Store Cafe retain the Beach community name.\nGet around.\nThere is no gas/fueling station on Lummi Island, so if you are taking your car over on the ferry, be sure you have enough fuel to get around.\nSee and do.\nThe island hosts a weekly farmers' market in the spring and summer, a chili festival in mid-July, and a Christmas party for island children.\nLummi Island is also home to many artists who conduct studio tours on Memorial Day, Labor Day, and the first weekend of December.\nIts narrow, scenic and winding roads are popular with bicyclers. \nA trail to Lummi Mountain takes hikers through the Baker Preserve to stunning high views of the San Juan and Gulf islands. The trail is maintained by the Lummi Island Heritage Trust."}
