{"id": "123297", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123297", "title": "Curitibanos", "text": "Curitibanos is in the Serrana region of Santa Catarina.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality is a major agricultural producer, with emphasis on the production of cereals such as soybeans and corn, fruit growing, especially apples, persimmons and peaches.\u00a0Also noteworthy is the production of garlic, which has already given it the title of \"national capital of garlic\"\nCattle\u00a0raising stands out."}
{"id": "123298", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123298", "title": "Pia\u00e7abu\u00e7u", "text": "Pia\u00e7abu\u00e7u is a beachside city in Alagoas, at the border with Sergipe, between the Atlantic Ocean and the S\u00e3o Francisco River, with a set of beautiful urban beaches. \nUnderstand.\nIts main source of income comes from primary activities, such as coconut farming, shrimp fishing and tourism. Pia\u00e7abu\u00e7u also has the largest shrimp bank in the Northeast and is an important fishing hub."}
{"id": "123299", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123299", "title": "Krishnagiri", "text": "Krishnagiri is a town in Northern Tamil Nadu.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBangalore Airport () is located approximately 90 km away.\nBy train.\nKrishnagiri is not connected to any railway line.\nBy bus.\nThere is a bus station in the north-west of the city at the Londenpet."}
{"id": "123303", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123303", "title": "Wybunbury", "text": "Wybunbury is a village in Cheshire.\nUnderstand.\nWybunbury (pronounced Win-bury in local accent) is a village about four miles from the railway town of Crewe, so is a popular destination for people who want to be near the railway but still want a quiet \"family holiday\" or day away from the hustle and bustle of the urban area.\nIt is located even closer to the market town of Nantwich.\nGet in.\nBy rail, take the railway to Crewe, and book either a taxi or take the bus to Wybunbury.\nGet around.\nBy foot, bus or even by horse and cow.\nSee.\nLots of dairy and beef farms in the area, so plenty of rural scenery.\nDo.\nAs a traditional rural village, there is plenty of sight seeing. A simple camera or smartphone can capture the local farms and wildlife quite easily.\nBuy.\nThere are local co-op shops in the village, to buy everyday essentials such as the newspaper and drinks. There is also a post office to deliver mail.\nConnect.\nAs a rural location internet access is not guaranteed. It is advisable you bring a smartphone or book a hotel with wifi access."}
