{"id": "123340", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123340", "title": "Guararema", "text": "Guararema is a small touristic town in Grande S\u00e3o Paulo. The city's government invested massively in tourism in the last few years, so there are several well-maintained attractions and charming places to eat at.\nUnderstand.\nFounded in the 17th century by the Catholic priests bringing converted native Brazilians, the area used to be a district of the city of Mogi das Cruzes. The train station, its central symbol, was a station in the stretch of Brazil Central Railway line (EFCB - Estrada de Ferro Central do Brasil) that connected Mogi das Cruzes with Jacare\u00ed, operating from 1876 to the 1970s."}
{"id": "123341", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123341", "title": "Jacare\u00ed", "text": "Jacare\u00ed is a city of 235,000 people (2020) in Grande S\u00e3o Paulo in Brazil. The city is known as the \"Capital of Beer\" by the daily output of its factories, considered the biggest in Latin America. \nUnderstand.\nThe economic activity is mainly based on industrial production. The industries produce mainly paper, chemicals, glass, wire, and rubber.\nClimate.\nThe city's climate is subtropical. The average annual temperature is 21.3 \u00b0C, with the coldest month being July (average of 17.5 \u00b0C) and the hottest month in February (average of 24.4 \u00b0C).\nGet around.\nJacare\u00ed's collective transport company Jacare\u00ed Transporte Urbano operates about 25 bus lines.\nDo.\nThe Cultural Foundation of Jacarehy \"Jos\u00e9 Maria de Abreu\" provides spaces for the artistic expression of the population.\nThe Parque da Cidade has a tennis court, indoor soccer and multi - sports courts, a walking track, gym equipment, stage for artistic presentations, exhibition grounds, and a bicycle path. Several gardens were built and around 100 species of trees were planted, including imperial palm, brazilwood and ironwood. \nJacare\u00ed has \u200b\u200ba professional football team, the Jacare\u00ed Atl\u00e9tico Clube, which competes in Series B of the Campeonato Paulista.\nBuy.\nJacare\u00ed also has a shopping mall with six movie theaters (two 2D and one 3D), one of which is the largest in the region. "}
{"id": "123343", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123343", "title": "Vaison-la-Romaine", "text": "Vaison-la-Romaine is in Vaucluse. The city is best known for its beautiful Roman remains including a single-arch bridge, and for its medieval city and its cathedral. What is unusual about a visit to Vaison is that you are able to see 2,000 years of history before your eyes \u2014 ancient, medieval and modern \u2014 in a single glance.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nVaison la Romaine is accessible via the Avignon-Caumont Airport, which is mainly served by low-cost airlines. The other nearest airports are Marseille Provence Airport and N\u00eemes-Garon Airport.\nBy train.\nThe nearest TGV station is Gare d'Avignon TGV, and other lines, the Orange.\nBy bus.\nSeveral bus lines connect Vaison la Romaine cities with the regional \"(bus line[dead link])\":\nBy car.\nVaison la Romaine is accessible by RD975 from Orange and exit of Highway A7 and Highway A9 #21 - Orange Centre, as well as the RD 938 from Carpentras.\nGet around.\nThe city does not have bus service. It is not extensive, and the modern and ancient parts are easy to walk. The medieval part is a little more strenuous to walk through, as it is on a hill dominated by the medieval castle. A tourist train from the tourist office offers a motorized guided tour that lasts 35 minutes and costs 4.50 \u20ac.\nSee.\nTwo of the most interesting aspects of the town are its geography and its ruins. The Roman ruins and the modern town are in the valley on the banks of a river which is crossed by an ancient bridge from the 1st century AD.\nThe medieval town is high on a rocky cliff. The valley floor was safe from attack in Roman and modern times. In the Middle Ages, attacks were frequent, and the town retreated uphill to a more defensible position.\nThe apse of the Church of St. Quenin, dedicated to Saint Quinidius, seems to date from the eighth century; it is one of the oldest in France. As a whole, the cathedral dates from the 11th century, but the apse and the apsidal chapels are from the Merovingian period.\nDo.\nWalk through the medieval city to the ruins of the castle. Please note that the narrow streets up are steep and that the last part is not paved. Once at the top you are rewarded with a magnificent view over the region.\nEat.\nYou can find several restaurants at Place Montfort."}
{"id": "123349", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123349", "title": "Arco (Idaho)", "text": "Arco is a city in Central Idaho. In 1955 Arco became the first city on Earth to be solely lit by nuclear energy.\nUnderstand.\nNearby Idaho National Laboratory, formerly known as National Reactor Testing Station and under various other names, is an important place for nuclear science and engineering development. Notable milestones are its first usable amount of electricity produced from nuclear power and the power plant for the world\u2019s first nuclear submarine. The laboratory was also a place of the US's first nuclear accident with fatalities in 1961. The Stationary Low-Power Reactor Number One (SL-1), a US Army experimental nuclear power reactor, underwent a steam explosion and meltdown, killing three operators."}
{"id": "123363", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123363", "title": "Santos Dumont", "text": "Santos Dumont is a city in the Southern Minas Gerais.\nUnderstand.\nSantos Dumont is the birthplace of aviation pioneer Alberto Santos-Dumont and is named in his honour. Prior to the renaming the city was known as Palmira. The Cabangu museum is dedicated to his memory. It is in the house where he was born."}
{"id": "123371", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123371", "title": "Ca\u00e7apava", "text": "Ca\u00e7apava is a city of 95,000 people (2020) in the North Coast region of S\u00e3o Paulo.\nUnderstand.\nClimate.\nThe climate of Ca\u00e7apava is tropical in altitude with dry winters. The warmest month is February, averaging highs of 28.1 \u00b0C, and the coldest month is July, averaging lows of 9.6 \u00b0C. The wettest month is January, with an average rainfall of 216.9 mm and the least rainy months are July and August with 32.9 and 35.3 mm, respectively.\nOn some winter days in the city, frost may occur, and often the thermal sensation in July can reach 1 \u00b0C. In summer, temperatures rise, ranging from 25 \u00b0C to 30 \u00b0C, making it a very hot summer."}
{"id": "123372", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=123372", "title": "Taubat\u00e9", "text": "Taubat\u00e9 is in the North Coast region of S\u00e3o Paulo.\nUnderstand.\nThe second largest city of the Para\u00edba Valley, Taubat\u00e9 was the home of actor and film director Am\u00e1cio Mazaroppi and writer Monteiro Lobato, two renowned personalities of Brazilian culture; there are museums dedicated to each. The city is also an excellent base to explore the North Coast region of the state.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe () shares its runway with a Brazilian Army aviation base, and receives no regular flights.\nBy bus.\nThe city has two bus terminals:"}
